  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Consider the polynomial $P(x)=x^4-5x^2+4x+13$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p30-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$P(x)$ can be expressed in the form $(x^2-a)^2+(x-b)^2$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=3$, $b=-2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x^2-a)^2+(x-b)^2&=x^4-2ax^2+a^2+x^2-2bx+b^2\\\\&=x^4+(1-2a)x^2-2bx+a^2+b^2.\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients:\n$$\\begin{align*}1-2a&=-5\\\\a&=3\\\\-2b&=4\\\\b&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe constant term checks: $3^2+(-2)^2=13$. Thus $P(x)=(x^2-3)^2+(x+2)^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why the graph of $P(x)$ has zero $x$-intercepts.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both squares are non-negative and cannot both be zero, so $P(x)>0$ for every real $x$. Hence there are no $x$-intercepts.",
          "worked": "$P(x)=(x^2-3)^2+(x+2)^2$. Each square is non-negative. Their sum could be zero only if $x^2-3=0$ and $x+2=0$ simultaneously. However, $x+2=0$ gives $x=-2$, for which $x^2-3=1$. Therefore $P(x)>0$ for every real $x$, so its graph has no $x$-intercepts.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
